# Which devices take their marks one at a time, and why.
#
# A method stated as a compiled operator can be called by the machinery
# that resolves marks: the span conversion fills through it, the image
# blit writes rows through it, and the glyph walk binds it once for a
# whole string. A method stated as a PostScript procedure can be called
# by nothing but the interpreter, so the machinery hands it the marks
# singly instead -- a call per span, per sample, per inked pixel.
#
# Each line below names a device and a slot that is not compiled, and is
# followed by the reason. tests/check-device-fastpaths.sh holds this file
# and the devices to each other: a slow slot with no line here fails, and
# a line here for a slot that is compiled fails too.
#
# A reason is why the method CANNOT be compiled, not that nobody has. The
# last group has no such reason and says so, with what it costs.

# ---- the writers: the method is the output language ------------------
#
# A mark reaching one of these becomes a drawing instruction in the
# language it writes. The method is where that language is spelled, and
# it is spelled in PostScript because what it emits is PostScript-shaped
# text. Compiling it would move the writer's syntax into C, which is the
# opposite of where this tree keeps a policy.

slow pdfwrite PutPix
slow pdfwrite GetPix
slow pdfwrite BlendPix
slow pdfwrite FillRect
slow pdfwrite FillPoly
slow svgwrite PutPix
slow svgwrite GetPix
slow svgwrite BlendPix
slow svgwrite FillRect
slow svgwrite FillPoly
slow dscwrite PutPix
slow dscwrite GetPix
slow dscwrite BlendPix
slow dscwrite FillRect
slow dscwrite FillPoly

# ---- bbox: it takes no marks, only their bounds ----------------------
#
# The extent device answers where the ink would be. It is handed a
# glyph's box rather than its pixels (GlyphExtents), and a mark it does
# take is folded into four numbers. There is no bulk route to want.

slow bbox PutPix
slow bbox GetPix
slow bbox BlendPix
slow bbox FillRect
slow bbox FillPoly

# ---- null: the marks are dropped before they are resolved ------------
#
# The device that draws nothing carries DiscardsMarks, and the machinery
# reads that entry and does not resolve the marks at all -- no sample is
# converted, no run is filled, no glyph raster is walked. Compiling these
# would make the calls cheap; not making them is cheaper still.

slow null PutPix
slow null GetPix
slow null BlendPix
slow null FillRect
slow null FillPoly

# ---- the pixel reader: not on a bulk path ---------------------------
#
# These four keep their raster as the array of row strings the base
# classes hold, and every method that takes marks in bulk is compiled
# against it -- the rectangle fill, the polygon fill, the blend and the
# pixel writer. GetPix is the odd one out and stays a procedure: nothing
# resolving marks reads a pixel back. It is asked for by a program, one
# call at a time by its nature, and a compiled one would answer the same
# question at the same rate.

slow pbm GetPix
slow pgm GetPix
slow ppm GetPix
slow tiff GetPix
